course: physics
lesson: 31
title: Energy: what makes things happen
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Energy is what lets things move, heat up, light up or change; you cannot see it, but you can see what it does.
explain: Your breakfast stores energy, and your body lets it out to run to school, to grow and to keep warm, which is why you are starving after a swim.
explain: Follow the energy back far enough and nearly all of it started in the Sun: plants catch sunlight to make food, animals eat plants, and even wind and rain on the hills are driven by the Sun's warmth.
explain: Energy is never used up, it only changes form: when a bouncing ball gets lower each bounce, the missing energy has turned into a little heat and sound.
explain: "Using energy" really means turning useful energy into heat that spreads out and is hard to use again.
fact: Energy is what lets things move, heat up, light up or change.
fact: Food stores energy that your body releases to move, grow and keep warm; most of it keeps you warm.
fact: Moving and keeping warm in cool water use lots of energy, and food labels count that energy, often in calories.
fact: Petrol burns inside a car engine and the hot gas pushes the parts that turn the wheels; electric cars store their energy in big batteries.
fact: Plants catch sunlight to make their food, and animals eat plants, so the energy in your breakfast started as sunlight.
fact: Coal, oil and gas are the remains of plants and tiny creatures that caught sunlight millions of years ago.
fact: A wind turbine turns the energy of moving air into electricity; the wind itself is made by the Sun warming the air unevenly.
fact: A solar panel turns light straight into electricity; it makes less on a cloudy day and none at night.
fact: Water held high behind a dam stores energy because of gravity, and the Sun lifted that water first as vapour that fell as rain.
fact: Energy can change form, but the total amount stays the same; it is never used up.
fact: A wind-up toy stores energy in a coiled spring, like a bent bow or a stretched rubber band.
fact: Each time a ball bounces, a little of its energy turns into heat and sound, so it never bounces back as high.
fact: Shivering is your muscles working fast to make extra heat.
exercise: Where does the energy for running to school come from? | The food you ate | The air you breathe out | The shoes on your feet | The ground pushing you along
exercise: Why does swimming make you so hungry? | Your muscles used lots of energy moving and keeping warm | Swallowing pool water stretches your tummy | The chlorine in the pool makes food smell nicer | Water washes the food out of your body
exercise: What is energy? | What lets things move, heat up, light up or change | A kind of invisible liquid that is stored in batteries | Another word for electricity only | A gas found only in food
exercise: Where does a petrol car get its energy? | Petrol burning in the engine | The air rushing past the car | The rubber in the tyres | The driver's foot on the pedal
exercise: Where do electric cars store their energy? | In big batteries | In a petrol tank | In their spare tyre | In the steering wheel
exercise: Where did the energy in your breakfast start? | As sunlight caught by plants | Inside the cold fridge at home | In the cooker's hot blue flame | In the salt that comes from the sea
exercise: What are coal, oil and gas made from? | Remains of plants and tiny creatures from millions of years ago | Rocks melted by volcanoes last year | Rain that soaked into the ground | Dinosaur bones only
exercise: What turns a wind turbine on a windy hill? | The wind pushing on its blades | A motor hidden inside the tower | Electricity from the town | The turning of the Earth
exercise: Where does the wind's energy really come from? | The Sun warming the air unevenly | The Moon pulling the air around the world | Trees waving their branches about | Waves on the sea blowing the air along
exercise: Why does a solar panel make less electricity on a cloudy day? | Less sunlight reaches it | Clouds make it too cold to work | Rain fills it up with water | The panel goes to sleep
exercise: How did the water behind a high dam first get up the hill? | The Sun lifted it as vapour, and it fell as rain | Pumps carried it up from the sea | It was always up there, ever since the Earth began | Rivers flow uphill in the mountains
exercise: When a rolling ball stops, what happened to its energy? | It turned into a little heat and sound | It was destroyed and is gone forever | It went back into the person who kicked it | It soaked into the ball and stays there
exercise: Where is the energy stored in a wind-up toy? | In a coiled spring inside | In a tiny battery in its feet | In the paint on its body | In the air inside the toy
exercise: Why does a bouncing ball get lower each bounce? | Some energy turns into heat and sound each time | Gravity gets a little stronger after every bounce | The ball gets heavier as it bounces | The floor steals the ball's air
exercise: Why does a squash ball get warm during a game? | Each bounce turns some energy into heat | The players keep breathing hot air on it | The court heats it up with big warm lamps | It rubs against the air just like a match does
exercise: Why do you shiver when you are cold? | Your muscles work fast to make extra heat | Your body is trying to shake off the cold air | Your bones are freezing and rattling | Your blood has stopped moving
test: ugh my teacher says energy never gets used up. but my phone battery literally died?? | The energy changed into other forms, mostly heat and light | Your teacher is wrong, energy gets destroyed | The energy is still in the battery, just hiding | The phone ate the energy and it's gone
test: Liam says his porridge is basically old sunshine. Is he right? | Yes, the oats grew by catching sunlight | No, porridge energy comes from the cooker | No, food has no energy at all | Yes, but only if he eats it outside
test: If the Sun went out, what would happen to wind power on Earth? | It would fail, because the Sun drives the wind | It would get stronger without sunshine | Nothing, the wind does not need the Sun | It would be the only kind of power left that still works
test: In a cartoon, a ball bounces forever just as high each time. What's wrong? | A real ball loses some energy as heat and sound each bounce | Nothing, balls bounce forever | Balls should bounce higher each time | Balls should stop after exactly one bounce
test: Gran sits still in her chair all day. Is her body using energy? | Yes, it turns food into heat to keep her warm | No, energy is only used up when you move about | No, old people do not use energy | Only when she is asleep
test: A wind-up mouse has stopped. How can you give it more energy? | Wind its spring up again | Put it in the sunshine | Leave it to rest overnight | Shake it hard
test: what's the point of a solar panel at night then, it's useless innit | At night it makes no electricity, which is why homes also use mains power or batteries | It makes even more at night from moonlight | It stores the dark for later | Panels work the same day and night
test: Coal, oil and gas are sometimes called "stored sunshine". Why? | They formed from living things that caught sunlight long ago | They glow faintly yellow in the dark | They are only dug up in sunny countries | They were melted together by the Sun
test: Mum says the water behind the dam is "stored energy". What does she mean? | Water up high can fall and spin a turbine | The water is full of tiny batteries, like a phone | The water is hot inside | The water is electric already
test: Why do you need more food on a cold day outside than on a warm day at home? | Your body uses more energy to keep warm | Cold air makes the food inside you smaller | Your tummy grows a lot bigger when it is cold | You lose food out through your skin in the cold
test: A bow and arrow and a wind-up toy store energy in the same kind of way. How? | Both store it by bending or coiling something springy | Both store it in a battery | Both store it as heat | Both store it in the air around them until it is needed
exam: Identify the original source of the energy contained within the greater part of human food. | Sunlight captured by plants | Heat derived from the cooker | Minerals dissolved in the soil | The motion of the oceans
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conservation of energy? | Energy may change its form, but its total quantity remains constant | Energy is consumed and destroyed by every machine | Energy is created anew whenever an object moves | Energy is lost entirely when a device ceases operation
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a bouncing ball attains a diminished height upon each successive rebound. | A portion of its energy is converted into heat and sound upon impact | The gravitational force increases after each impact | The mass of the ball increases upon contact with the floor | The air within the ball is extracted by the floor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of wind energy. | Unequal heating of the atmosphere by the Sun | The gravitational attraction of the Moon | The rotation of the turbine blades themselves | The motion of trees and vegetation
